From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lierfang Support Team <itsupport@lierfang.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ACPI: processor_idle: fix missing semicolon after
 ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE()

The aosc/v6.18.62 tree rewrote the ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE() call in
acpi_idle_enter() to use braces but dropped the trailing semicolon:

	if (cx->type == ACPI_STATE_C3) {
		ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE()
	}

On x86 the macro expands to a do { ... } while (0) block
(arch/x86/include/asm/acenv.h), so the statement needs the semicolon and
the build fails:

  drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c: In function 'acpi_idle_enter':
  drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c:700:9: error: expected ';' before '}' token
  drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c:707:22: error: invalid storage class for
  function 'acpi_idle_enter_s2idle'
  [... every following function in the file ...]
  drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c:1472:1: error: expected declaration or
  statement at end of input

Only the first error is real; acpi_idle_enter() never closes, so the
compiler treats the rest of the file as nested inside it.

The two other call sites in the same file (lines 585 and 728) already
have the semicolon. The generic ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE() in
include/acpi/platform/aclinux.h expands to nothing, which is why this
only breaks on x86.

Upstream v6.18 has no braces here:

	if (cx->type == ACPI_STATE_C3)
		ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE();

Keep the braces and just add the semicolon, so the fix stays a one
character diff against the tree we build.

Signed-off-by: Lierfang Support Team <itsupport@lierfang.com>
---
 drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

Index: b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
===================================================================
--- a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
@@ -696,7 +696,7 @@
 	}
 
 	if (cx->type == ACPI_STATE_C3) {
-		ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE()
+		ACPI_FLUSH_CPU_CACHE();
 	}
 
 	acpi_idle_do_entry(cx);
